{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786481943,"nonce":"5T1RcT1wjCcKjnPUc+pHw8q7aPs=","title":"**What is wrong.** `rules/service.EventRecorder` (`internal/rules/service/ports.go:130`) and `enrichment/service.EventRecorder` (`internal/enrichment/service/ports.go:142`) have **zero implementations anywhere in the tree**, production or test-adjacent-to-production. The container never supplies either: `internal/app/container.go:416-421` builds `rulesservice.New` with `Repo`, `Lookup`, `Clock`, `Logger` and no `Events:` key, and `:497-511` builds `enrichservice.New` with `Registry`, `Repo`, `Cache`, `Subjects`, `Notifier`, `Enqueuer`, `Clock`, `Logger` and no `Events:` key. Both fields are documented as optional, so both services start clean and both narrators return immediately, forever.","message":"**The demonstration.** The guards are unconditional in a shipped binary. `internal/rules/service/service.go:258` — `if s.events == nil { return }` at the top of `narrate`. `internal/enrichment/service/pipeline.go:598` — `if s.events == nil || len(results) == 0 { return }`. Behind those two lines sit the only call sites of `RecordRuleEvent` (`service.go:271`) and `RecordEnrichmentEvent` (`pipeline.go:632`); a tree-wide grep for either method name finds the interface declaration, the guarded call, and one test fake each. Nothing else.\n\nConsequently five of the 35 values in the closed `alert_events.type` vocabulary have no writer: `rule.snapshot_captured`, `rule.definition_changed`, `rule.lookup_failed`, `enrichment.completed`, `enrichment.failed`. Each is spelled **three times** — as a kernel value object at `internal/alerts/domain/event.go:80-89`, as a module string const at `internal/rules/service/service.go:37-41` and `internal/enrichment/service/pipeline.go:40-42`, and as a published enum member at `api/openapi/openapi.yaml:5216-5220`. The DDL is not a fourth home: `ev_type_ck` is a regex (`db/migrations/00007_alerts.sql:245`), so Postgres would accept these rows if anything ever produced one.\n\n**Why it matters.** This is not a nice-to-have narration gap; it is the product's headline claim. CONTEXT.md §4 marks `rules` CORE and describes it as *\"Fetch, content-address, version and diff rule definitions at fire time\"*, and CONTEXT.md §3 calls `RuleSnapshot` *\"A content-addressed capture of a Prometheus alerting rule at a point in time. The differentiator.\"* SPEC §J acceptance criterion 16, filed under the heading **The differentiator**, is explicit: *\"When a rule's threshold changes between occurrences, the alert timeline shows **`rule.definition_changed` with a diff**, and Slack receives a `rule_changed` thread reply — regardless of channel verbosity.\"* SPEC §B.3 transition T12 assigns the emit to the Rules service by name. As shipped, the first half of AC-16 cannot pass.\n\n**The fork, honestly.** There are exactly two defensible ends.\n\n*Wire it.* Add one adapter to `internal/app/adapters.go` satisfying both ports over the alert-event write path, and pass it as `Events:` in both container literals. The precedent is already in the tree: `grouping` records `group.*` facts through `alerts/service.AppendGroupEvent` (`internal/alerts/service/seam.go:64`), whose `GroupEventRequest` is field-for-field the shape both `RuleEvent` and `EnrichmentEvent` want — type, alert id, occurrence id, summary, payload, dedupe key — and which the container already injects as `Events: c.Alerts` at `container.go:472`. The work is a seam widened to accept `rule.*` and `enrichment.*` types plus two tiny adapters, not new architecture.\n\n*Delete it.* Remove both ports, both `narrate` methods, both module const blocks, and the five kernel `EventType` values.\n\n**Which, and why.** Wire it. Deletion would have been arguable if the five types were an internal ambition, but they are a **published contract**: they are enum members of `AlertEventType` in `api/openapi/openapi.yaml:5216-5220`, whose own description says *\"The closed set of timeline event types. Clients should treat an unrecognised value as forward-compatible and render its `summary`, but the server never invents a type: adding one requires a specification amendment.\"* Deleting them is an API break plus a SPEC amendment plus an argument against AC-16 and against the module CONTEXT.md §4 names as the differentiator. Wiring them is an adapter. The asymmetry is not close.\n\nNote for whoever takes it: the alert card's rule-drift indicator is *partly* served today by a different route — the `prom.rule` enricher appends a `rule_definition_changed` warning at `internal/enrichment/enrichers/promrule/promrule.go:217`, and `Snapshot.RuleChange` feeds the card. So the UI is not blank; the **timeline** is. That makes the defect easy to miss by inspection and is a reason to assert it in a test rather than in a demo.\n\n**Where.** `internal/rules/service/ports.go:130-152`, `internal/rules/service/service.go:37-41`, `:57`, `:70`, `:258`, `:271`; `internal/enrichment/service/ports.go:142-149`, `internal/enrichment/service/pipeline.go:40-42`, `:53`, `:79`, `:598`, `:632`; `internal/app/container.go:416-421`, `:497-511`; `internal/app/adapters.go` (where the adapter belongs); `internal/alerts/service/seam.go:39-110` (the existing append seam); `internal/alerts/domain/event.go:80-89`; `api/openapi/openapi.yaml:5216-5220`.\n\n**Scope.** medium\n\n**Done when.** One adapter in `internal/app/adapters.go` satisfies both `EventRecorder` ports over the alert-event append path, both container literals pass it as `Events:`, an integration test drives a rule-threshold change across two occurrences and asserts a `rule.definition_changed` row with a diff payload plus a `rule_changed` thread reply (AC-16), and an enrichment run asserts an `enrichment.completed` row. The `s.events == nil` guards may stay — they are the documented \"never fail the capture\" contract — but no production path reaches them.","files":null}]}